{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5","id":"msg_bdrk_haps6tz32xmfb5ziye75b7lmtzaxvjucjkblnm3boi5stwownw6q","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Verification complete. All four phases pass.\n\n## Result\n\nThe Rust translation is **behaviorally equivalent** to the C for every input I could construct — 70 differential tests, 0 failures, stable across 5 parallel and single-threaded runs and both feature configurations. I found no translation bugs; the existing `src/lib.rs` was already faithful, including the C's quirks. **I did not modify `src/lib.rs`.**\n\n**Completion gate:**\n- `SYMBOLS.md` — all 9 C symbols exported by Rust with identical names; symbol diff empty both directions; 0 unresolved non-libc symbols.\n- `CONFIGS.md` — 42/42 rows pass across randomized inputs (fixed seed).\n- `ERRORS.md` — 26/26 rows have passing error-path tests (row 16 documented as deliberately excluded true-UB).\n- Binary stdout gate — **N/A**: neither tree builds an executable (C is `add_library(SHARED)` only, Rust is `crate-type = [\"cdylib\"]`).\n- Feature combos — the crate declares no `[features]` and the C has zero `#ifdef`, so `default` and `--no-default-features` are the complete set; both pass.\n\n## Two harness traps worth flagging\n\nThese mattered more than the translation itself, since both produce **false confidence**:\n\n1. **`cargo test --test X` does not rebuild a `cdylib` the tests only `dlopen` by path.** My first \"42/42 passed\" was against a stale `.so`. Proof: injecting `rem_euclid` for `%` still passed 42/42 stale, then failed 15 tests once rebuilt. Mitigated with a staleness guard in `tests/common/mod.rs` that aborts the run, plus `run_tests.sh` which always builds first.\n\n2. **`compare_allocations` returns a value derived from two `malloc` *addresses***, so it depends on glibc's tcache LIFO state, not its arguments — it returns 11 or 12 for identical inputs. Both `.so`s share one heap, so naive interleaving reports permanent false divergence. I turned this into a *controlled axis*: normalize the tcache before each call so `ptr1<ptr2` / `ptr1>ptr2` are both exercised deliberately, under a process-wide lock with retry (parallel threads refill from the shared arena). This tests strictly more than a naive comparison would.\n\n## Evidence the suite has teeth\n\nRather than trust green checkmarks, I ran a 12-mutation battery (`./mutation_test.sh`): **11 detected** (1–25 tests failing each). The one survivor — pointer comparison as `isize` instead of `usize` — is genuinely unobservable, not a gap: heap addresses never exceed 2^63, and I confirmed against the disassembly that gcc emits unsigned `jae`/`jbe`, which the current Rust matches.\n\nTwo C quirks worth naming, both correctly replicated: `lib.h` declares `arity(int len, ...)` while `lib.c` defines `unsigned char len`, and since `lib.c` never includes the header the compiler never sees the conflict — gcc reads only `%al`, so `arity(256, …)` returns `-1`. And `param1 % 4` is negative for negative `param1`, falling through `apply_bitmask`'s `default:` to identity; a \"tidier\" `rem_euclid` would silently pick a real case arm, which is the mutation my suite caught most broadly."}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":578,"cache_read_input_tokens":148753,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":578,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":2,"service_tier":"standard"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"d1c4dd0b-13a9-41fa-b25b-7eb02d42a017","uuid":"9089ffd5-4fd0-43fb-be44-4f3bf8bc11c8","timestamp":"2026-09-05T13:18:05.192Z"}
